    "knowledge_point": "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Cosine Function",
    "problem": "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$\n\n- $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\n- $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\n- $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$",
    "worked_solution": "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ does **not** lie within this range.   $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$",
